\section{Physical Inductive Biases Fail}
\label{sec:fails}

We inject physical structure into the shared $192$-dimensional latent through five mechanisms spanning the design space---from pinning \emph{what the state is} to constraining \emph{how it evolves}, with and without labels (Table~\ref{tab:mechanisms}). All variants run on top of free rollout, i.e., they must beat an already strong training protocol, which is the deployment-relevant question.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.1cm}p{2.5cm}cc@{}}
\toprule
Mechanism & Constrains & Labels & Form \\
\midrule
Fixed slot & state value ($\latp{=}$pos.) & yes & --- \\
Probe & state readout & yes & --- \\
Kinematic head & evolution & yes & fixed/MLP \\
Consistency & evolution (velocity) & yes & form-free \\
Label-free prior & evolution & \textbf{no} & 2nd-order \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Five physical-structure injection mechanisms. ``Fixed/MLP'' includes the exact gravitational form $a{=}g$ with a single learnable parameter. The label-free prior requires the slot to evolve under smooth second-order dynamics without ground-truth grounding.}
\label{tab:mechanisms}
\end{table}

\subsection{Post-hoc Injection: Every Mechanism Hurts}
\label{sec:posthoc}

Table~\ref{tab:injection} reports the hardest-split latent nMSE for each mechanism against the pure free-rollout baseline. Three observations stand out. First, \emph{correct physics does not help}: on parabola, the strict gravitational head---the ground-truth functional form, with only the gravity constant learned---degrades error from $0.313$ to $0.372$, worse even than an unconstrained MLP head. Second, \emph{labels do not help}: the label-free prior degrades all three domains (collision most, $0.393\!\to\!0.653$), but so does its fully grounded counterpart with perfect position labels ($0.131\!\to\!0.166$ on uniform). Third, \emph{combinations do not rescue}: probe supervision alone is harmful ($0.131\!\to\!0.167$; confirmed in pixel space, $20.41\!\to\!19.83$\,dB on both-OOD), and adding it to the best slot configuration drags that configuration down. Extending the slot to also carry velocity is the worst variant of all ($0.114\!\to\!0.207$). The form-free consistency loss, designed specifically for collision impulses, fails worst exactly there ($0.57$--$0.64$ vs.\ $0.393$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
 & uniform & parabola & collision \\
\midrule
Free-rollout baseline & $0.131$ & $0.313$ & $0.393$ \\
\midrule
Fixed slot (structpos) & $0.183$ & --- & --- \\
Probe ($\lambda{=}1$, $K{=}2$) & $0.167$ & --- & --- \\
Kinematic (free MLP) & $0.206^{*}$ & $0.354$ & worse \\
Kinematic (strict $a{=}g$) & $0.206$ & $0.372$ & --- \\
Consistency (form-free) & --- & $0.291$ & $0.57$--$0.64$ \\
Label-free prior & $0.171$ & $0.359$ & $0.653$ \\
Grounded prior & $0.166$ & $0.392$ & --- \\
Slot$+$probe combined & $0.125$ & --- & --- \\
Slot carrying pos$+$vel & $0.207$ & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Post-hoc physical-structure injection, hardest-split latent nMSE ($\downarrow$; both-OOD for uniform/collision, clean partitions for parabola where applicable). Every mechanism degrades every domain it was run on. Single-seed cells; the baseline and the load-bearing variant are seed-replicated in Tables~\ref{tab:headline} and~\ref{tab:lbr}. $^{*}$MLP and strict forms coincide on uniform ($a{=}0$).}
\label{tab:injection}
\end{table}

\subsection{From-Scratch Co-Training Hurts More}
\label{sec:scratch}

A natural defense holds that physical structure cannot be grafted onto an encoder already committed to a black-box representation---it must be baked in during pretraining, as extrinsic architectures do. We test this with a $2{\times}2$ factorial (init $\in$ \{PushT post-hoc, from-scratch\} $\times$ physics $\in$ \{off, on\}) per domain, with learning rate re-tuned so the from-scratch baselines converge cleanly. The defense fails, and in the strongest possible way (Table~\ref{tab:scratch}): from-scratch physics is \emph{more} damaging than post-hoc grafting. On uniform motion, the physics penalty grows from $+0.035$ (post-hoc) to $+0.558$ (from-scratch)---the cleaner the baseline, the larger the harm. All three domains show positive penalties in both regimes; the ``inject at pretraining'' escape hatch is closed.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
Domain & Split & phys.\ off & phys.\ on & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{uniform} & scratch & $0.192$ & $0.750$ & $\mathbf{+0.558}$ \\
 & post-hoc & $0.131$ & $0.166$ & $+0.035$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{parabola (\rmood)} & scratch & $0.343$ & $0.375$ & $+0.03$ \\
 & post-hoc & $0.124$ & $0.198$ & $+0.07$ \\
\midrule
collision & scratch & $0.538$ & $0.635$ & $+0.097$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Pretraining vs.\ post-hoc physics injection (latent nMSE $\downarrow$, hardest clean split; strict $a{=}g$ head $+$ fixed slot). Physics hurts in every cell, and most where the baseline is cleanest. Parabola is judged on \rmood{} due to the denominator pathology of its both-OOD aggregate (\S\ref{sec:traps}).}
\label{tab:scratch}
\end{table}

\subsection{The Load-Bearing Problem}
\label{sec:lbr}

Why does structure fail so uniformly? The physical slot occupies $2$ of $192$ dimensions; under a mean prediction loss it receives ${\sim}1\%$ of the gradient, so the predictor pays almost nothing for getting it wrong. Meanwhile the remaining $190$ black-box dimensions \emph{redundantly encode position}---a ridge probe reads position from them at $\rho{>}0.9$---so prediction and decoding can route around the slot entirely. The slot aligns almost perfectly with true position (its supervision loss falls from $2.53$ to $0.015$) while contributing nothing: it is decodable but not load-bearing. Worse, the auxiliary gradients actively compete with prediction: at probe weight $\lambda{=}10$ the probe-to-prediction gradient ratio reaches $15$--$125\times$ across domains and the encoder's effective dimensionality collapses by $39$--$90\%$.

This account makes a falsifiable prediction: re-weighting the prediction loss so the slot dimensions carry weight (load-bearing reweighting, LBR; weight ${\sim}30$ on the slot) should remove the damage. It does (Table~\ref{tab:lbr}): the harmful fixed slot ($0.183$) returns exactly to baseline parity ($0.132\pm0.014$ vs.\ $0.136\pm0.007$ over three seeds)---and no further. The surviving benefits are narrow and metric-dependent: with a kinematic head on the now load-bearing slot, velocity-OOD decoded position reaches $\rho{=}0.99$ and long-horizon pixel accuracy improves by $+1.25$\,dB on uniform (single-seed), only in smooth domains, and at the cost of degraded zero-shot transfer ($0.551$, the worst of all variants). Structure, even when made load-bearing, buys interpretability rather than net robustness---consistent with attributing the reported success of extrinsic architectures \citep{mallari2024piwm} to their \emph{architecture} (physical state as the sole, unavoidable prediction pathway) rather than to physical equations.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Configuration (uniform) & both-OOD nMSE & seeds \\
\midrule
Free-rollout baseline & $0.136\pm0.007$ & 3 \\
Fixed slot, weight $1$ & $0.183$ & 1 \\
Fixed slot $+$ LBR ($w{=}30$) & $0.132\pm0.014$ & 3 \\
Fixed slot $+$ LBR ($w{=}100$) & $0.136$ & 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Load-bearing reweighting (LBR) restores parity, not gains: the mechanism's falsifiable prediction is confirmed---making the slot load-bearing removes its harm---but structure yields no net OOD improvement.}
\label{tab:lbr}
\end{table}

