\section{What Actually Helps: Training and Data}
\label{sec:works}

\subsection{Free Rollout Dominates}
\label{sec:fr}

Replacing teacher forcing with free rollout is the single largest and most portable improvement we find (Table~\ref{tab:headline}): $2.2\times$ on uniform, $3.6\times$ on parabola, and $2.4\times$ on collision, with three-seed intervals that do not overlap in any domain. The diagnosis matches the classic exposure-bias account \citep{bengio2015scheduled,venkatraman2015improving}: under teacher forcing, one-step predictions are near-perfect (cosine $0.98$--$0.99$) while multi-step rollouts drift at a rate ordered by dynamics complexity (uniform $<$ parabola $<$ collision)---the failure is invisible at training time and catastrophic at rollout time. The result carries to real video: on Physion++, free rollout is the main long-horizon lever (horizon-$32$ cosine $0.91$ vs.\ $0.79$--$0.87$ for every physics-augmented variant), and among all synthetic-training configurations it transfers best to Physion OCP.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
 & teacher forcing & free rollout & gain \\
\midrule
uniform & $0.300\pm0.006$ & $\mathbf{0.136\pm0.007}$ & $2.2\times$ \\
parabola (\rmood) & $0.443\pm0.047$ & $\mathbf{0.122\pm0.006}$ & $3.6\times$ \\
collision & $1.153\pm0.040$ & $\mathbf{0.479\pm0.064}$ & $2.4\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Headline result: free rollout vs.\ teacher forcing, hardest clean split, latent nMSE ($\downarrow$), three seeds each (mean$\pm$std). Intervals do not overlap in any domain.}
\label{tab:headline}
\end{table}

\subsection{Match the Horizon to Dynamics Complexity}
\label{sec:horizon}

The rollout horizon $H$ is not a free win; it must match the domain. Collision improves markedly at $H{\approx}20$ (both-OOD $0.393\!\to\!0.294$; horizon-$28$ cosine $0.58\!\to\!0.70$)---the training window must contain post-impulse futures for the model to learn them. Smooth domains show the opposite: at $H{=}16$, uniform's long-horizon cosine drops from $0.969$ to $0.814$ and parabola degrades on every partition; excess horizon destabilizes amplitudes where $H{=}8$ already spans the dynamics. On real video the appetite is larger still: Physion++ error falls monotonically through $H\in\{8,16,20,28\}$, reaching horizon-$64$ nMSE $0.014$ vs.\ $0.280$ for the $H{=}8$ baseline ($19\times$) with no inflection found---real, multi-event dynamics reward the longest horizons we could train. The practical law: set the training horizon to cover the domain's causal event span, and no further than its stability allows.

\subsection{Augmentation: Strongest Synthetic Lever, Reversed on Real Video}
\label{sec:aug}

On synthetic domains, simple video-only augmentation beats every physical-structure method. Per-trial appearance jitter (brightness/contrast, strength $0.5$) targets the appearance shift induced by radius/mass changes and roughly halves the hardest split: uniform $0.131\!\to\!0.068$ ($-48\%$), parabola $-63\%$ (with the clean \rmood{} partition confirming, $0.127\!\to\!0.065$). Geometric scale jitter targets object size directly and, \emph{only} in combination with a long horizon, sets the collision record ($0.393\!\to\!0.172$; three seeds ${\approx}0.21$). The combinations are non-monotone and must be chosen, not stacked: appearance conflicts with long-horizon training ($0.472$, worse than either alone), scale synergizes with it ($0.208$), and the triple combination is worse than the best pair ($0.253$). Velocity-OOD resists all of it---temporal-stride augmentation, the natural candidate, loses even to appearance jitter ($0.556$ vs.\ $0.376$)---marking unseen velocities as the open hard case.

The synthetic recipe does not survive contact with real video. On Physion++, the same appearance augmentation that halves synthetic OOD error \emph{degrades friction-collision prediction by two orders of magnitude} (nMSE $0.062\!\to\!6.44$) while the latent cosine \emph{rises}---a preview of the metric traps in \S\ref{sec:traps}---and it does not help zero-shot Physion transfer either ($0.597$, below the $0.607$ random-prior ceiling). The boundary is principled: augmentation encodes an invariance assumption, and appearance is nuisance in renderer-generated scenes but signal in real ones (friction, mass, and material are read off appearance). Augmentation gains are domain-specific claims, not portable ones.

